<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Joseph Priestley</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1733">1733</a>–<a href="/years/1804">1804</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Priestley</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Joseph Priestley was an English chemist, natural philosopher, Unitarian theologian and political theorist who published over 150 works. He is credited with the independent discovery of oxygen, which he isolated in 1774 and called dephlogisticated air, and with the invention of carbonated water but was isolated by his defence of phlogiston theory. He argued for equal rights for religious Dissenters, helped found <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unitarianism">Unitarianism</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and wrote the Essay on the First Principles of Government (1768). In 1791, after a mob burned down his Birmingham home and church, he fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and then to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, settling in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northumberland">Northumberland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/joseph-priestley.jpg" alt="Joseph Priestley" width="330" height="449" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: James Sharples · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Joseph_Priestley_(NPG).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Priestley was born on 24 March 1733 into an established Dissenting family of the West Riding of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorkshire">Yorkshire</a>, the eldest of six children of Mary Swift and Jonas Priestley, a finisher of cloth. After his mother died he was raised partly by his aunt and uncle, who sought the best education for him with the ministry in view; at four he is said to have recited all 107 questions and answers of the Westminster Shorter Catechism. In 1752 he entered the Dissenting academy at Daventry, where he became a Rational Dissenter, and David Hartley's Observations on Man influenced him more than any book but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a>. His first post, at Needham Market in 1755, went badly because the congregation disliked his heterodoxy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1761 he became tutor of modern languages and rhetoric at the Warrington Academy, where he wrote on history and met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josiah-wedgwood">Josiah Wedgwood</a>. He moved to Leeds in 1767 as minister of Mill Hill Chapel and wrote his Essay on the First Principles of Government (1768) and Institutes of Natural and Revealed Religion (1772–74), which became standard for British Unitarians. From 1773 he was librarian and tutor to Lord Shelburne at Calne, where his experiments on airs led to his isolating oxygen in 1774 and to the six volumes of Experiments and Observations on Different Kinds of Air (1774–86). On 17 April 1774 his friend Theophilus Lindsey held the first Unitarian service in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, and Priestley supported him.</p><p>In 1780 he became minister at New Meeting in Birmingham, where he joined the Lunar Society and published An History of the Corruptions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> (1782). In July 1791 a dinner marking Bastille Day provoked a mob, which burned the New Meeting and Old Meeting churches and his house at Sparkbrook, destroying his laboratory; twenty-six other Dissenters' homes and three more churches burned in the three-day riot. He settled in Hackney, and on 4 June 1794 reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>. He built a home in Northumberland County, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>, and his sermons in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> led to the First Unitarian Church of Philadelphia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Priestley died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northumberland">Northumberland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>, on 6 February 1804. By then he belonged to every major scientific society in the Western world. Georges Cuvier called him the father of modern chemistry who never acknowledged his daughter, because he refused to abandon the phlogiston theory. His metaphysical works served as sources for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utilitarianism">utilitarianism</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-jefferson">Thomas Jefferson</a> said he rested the basis of his own faith on Priestley's books on Christian history.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Joseph Priestley” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By heating mercuric oxide, <b>Joseph Priestley</b> produced a gas in which a candle burned with great vigour.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-1">1 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
